  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 224 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe long term forecast is a little messy. The upper \nlevel low that gave us rain showers this weekend is expected to be \npushing into the Northern Plains/Midwest by Monday. This will cause \nan area of high pressure to reside over the Intermountain West \neventually building into more of a negatively canted ridge. Theres \nis also an area of high pressure that begins to form over the Great \nLakes region as our former low pressure system enters the Northern \nplains causing a traffic jam over the east coast. This becomes our \nproblem as the ridge builds over us it blocks the weak low pressure \nsystem off the west coast. Temperatures will start to climb at the \n700mb level raising the temperature from 6C back to 12C thus raising \nour cool fall-like surface temps back into the more summer-ish 70's \nand 80's. This upper level low is depicted to sit and spin sending a \nlittle bit of moisture and some weak shortwaves through Wednesday \nnight for some isolated to scattered thunderstorms and showers in \nthe afternoon and evenings, but with the weak ridge and drier \nairmass moving over us this week. Don't expect to have strong \nthunderstorm or scattered heavy rainfall chances. By Thursday, the \nIntermountain west really locks into a dry southwest flow pulling \ndry area from the desert of California and Arizona.",
